WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.158
WHIT STILLMAN: I'd like to
mention that four of our films

00:00:08.158 --> 00:00:09.200
were actually profitable.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.825
So it might be against
your principles.

00:00:10.825 --> 00:00:12.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.770
One was unprofitable.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:18.430
It's the one with 1,000
extras at a disco.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.090
But Warner Brothers
paid for that,

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.548
so we don't have
to worry about it.

00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:22.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:29.110
And so, if we do decide to
do film investing together,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.600
I'd like to mention
how we do it.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.650
To show that our
interests are aligned,

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.040
I put in a certain
investment, like 10,000,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.750
into the investment vehicle.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.080
And then you put in your $10,000
in the investment vehicle.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.050
And do any of you know con
where the guy does the rolled

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.837
up dollars under the arm
and the old, in the 70s?

00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:51.670
That was done a lot.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:52.780
It's not that.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.820
It's actually an honest thing.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.000
And I thought maybe the first
project we could do together

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.430
would be President Arnn's,
Churchill's Trials, Churchill's

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:02.930
Trial.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.690
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:06.620
[CLEARS THROAT]

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.410
Because Churchill
movies are a genre.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:10.302
They're popular.

00:01:10.302 --> 00:01:11.510
I've always wanted to do one.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:12.590
I can never get that job.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.570
They get English
people to do it.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.850
And there's just three
sections of the book.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:18.860
So it could be a series.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.880
We do the first one, The
War, which is commercial.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.290
And if The War works,
then we do the other two.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.260
So that's how it works
in the film business.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.660
You have to do sort of
a genre, or a series,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.200
or something like that.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.140
Anyway, I have to say, I
think there's a big problem

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.150
with this conference.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.280
There's far too much
emphasis on Jane Austen.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:46.280
It gets repetitive.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.640
The private theatricals
at Mansfield Park

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.920
have been worked to death.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.770
So, tonight I prefer to
talk about James Bond.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.750
The James Bond novels have
also been made into films.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:08.500
And so, first I'd like
to do a kind of survey.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.800
Who here has not seen
a James Bond film?

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.090
You've not seen one?

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.590
Never?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.030
Wow.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.730
Who is seeing the
old James Bond films

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.340
with Sean Connery
and Roger Moore?

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:23.990
Great, good, good, good.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.890
Who's read a James Bond novel?

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.570
OK, OK.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:30.980
So now we've done that.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.230
So how did Bond become a craze?

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.510
Ian Fleming's--
a bit of history.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:43.370
Ian Fleming's first novel Casino
Royale was published in 1953

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.920
and was moderately
successful in Britain.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.680
But in the US, where three
publishers had wisely turned it

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.720
down, it ended up flopping.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.860
The next year, 1954,
the CBS Network

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.300
did a one-hour TV version.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.320
But they put it into
an anthology series.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.670
In 1955 film rights to
Casino Royale were sold.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.190
But nothing came of that sell.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.580
Dr. No, the sixth novel
was published in 1958,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:13.870
and it's a really good book.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.730
I recommend it.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.590
Paul Johnson, who
became famous later

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.870
as a conservative,
popular historian,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.820
was then a man of
the left writing

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.440
in the New Statesman in London.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:33.730
And he excoriated Doctor No for
its sex, snobbery, and sadism.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.250
One would think that would have
piqued Hollywood's interest.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.720
But the novel went unsold.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.590
It was only the seventh
Bond novel, Goldfinger,

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.400
that in 1961 led Canadian
producer, Harry Saltzman,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.320
and American, "Cubby"
Broccoli, both

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.430
based in Europe to license the
James Bond character for films.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.540
Several Hollywood studios turned
the project down as too British

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.790
or too blatantly sexual.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:02.350
In those days
Hollywood didn't want

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:08.860
to do blatantly sexual
films, just the obvious ones,

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.030
with blatant, no.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.680
Eventually, the
independent-minded studio,

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.550
United Artists--

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.079
United Artists is
a great studio.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.260
It was started by DW
Griffith, and Mary Pickford,

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.089
and Douglas Fairbanks
back in the old days

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:23.530
to make their own films.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:27.400
And it's maintained a tradition
until recent decades of really

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.620
being the friendly studio
for independent projects

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.420
and the interesting projects.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:35.800
So they agreed to fund Dr. No
as a modestly, budgeted film.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.720
They really had a modest budget.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.210
Four film directors
turned the film down,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.490
perhaps because of
the modest budget.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:46.000
Anglo-Irish writer, director,
Terence Young signed on.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.720
And he sought to
make the film funny,

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.630
as he considered that
quote "A lot of things

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.120
in this film, the
sex, and violence,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.310
and so on, if played straight,
would A, be objectionable, B,

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:02.380
would never go past the censors.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.570
But the moment you make
it tongue in cheek,

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.360
it seems to disarm."

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.640
Corners were cut,
ingeniously, to keep the film

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.230
within its tight budget.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.640
Dr. No's fabulous aquarium,
fabulous aquarium,

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.110
was actually magnified
stock footage of goldfish.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.990
And they actually couldn't
afford stock footage

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.875
of scarier fish than goldfish.

00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:25.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.840
The scarier footage costs more.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.680
But the film was a big success,
and the rest is history.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.870
Except, not really.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:38.750
The films maintain their
glamorous, humorous charm

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.570
through Sean Connery
and Roger Moore films.

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.690
But in the decades since then
have become increasingly grim,

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:48.410
action films just like
other grim, action films.

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.810
But the initial critical
reaction to Dr. No

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.360
was not unmixed, which
means it was mixed.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:54.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.850
Time called Bond a
blithering bounder.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:05.120
Where does Time get
off to snub James Bond?

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.950
A blithering bounder.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:10.130
And a great-big,
hairy marshmallow.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.130
I mean, you see Sean
Connery as James Bond,

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.010
you think great-big,
hairy marshmallow.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.100
Maybe marshmallow meant
something different than.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:18.600
OK.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:19.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.610
Oh, he almost always manages
to seem slightly silly.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:24.470
Well, anyway.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.900
The New Republic said it
never decides whether it's

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.870
suspense or suspense spoof.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:34.820
The Vatican condemned the
film as a dangerous mixture

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.450
of violence, vulgarity,
sadism, and sex.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.790
The Vatican, I
think was premature.

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:44.900
Because I don't think Dr.
No-- well, I guess it was.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.960
But they'd definitely be
correct about the current films.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.860
Vatican is on target.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.860
The Kremlin said that Bond
was the personification

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.020
of capitalist evil.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.530
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:01.520
So I'm surprised everyone
here has not seen those films.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.316
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.290
Both these controversies
only helped

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.170
increase public awareness of
the film and greater cinema

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:14.680
attendance.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:15.938
Thanks, Wikipedia.

00:07:15.938 --> 00:07:16.855
That's from Wikipedia.

00:07:16.855 --> 00:07:18.200
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.000
So what is this to
do with Jane Austen?

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.030
Well, from the film
industry point of view

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:29.140
Jane Austen's novels are
like Bond novel's material.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.450
And normally no one is
interested in material

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.610
until someone else
takes a chance with it,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.600
and it turns out well.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.130
Then some good
works are created.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:39.840
But after a while
it becomes a genre.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.780
And once something becomes a
genre, next it becomes a glut.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.240
It begins to verge on
and more than verge on,

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.842
parody and travesty.

00:07:46.842 --> 00:07:49.050
I worry that we're going to
make the world thoroughly

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.558
sick of Jane Austen, which
would be a great shame.

00:07:51.558 --> 00:07:53.100
Because she has
enormous value for us

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.640
beyond being a purveyor of plot
lines for a romantic-period

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.400
yarns.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.750
And where am I?

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.350
Ch-ch-ch.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:09.544
Oh, the next paragraph.

00:08:09.544 --> 00:08:10.044
[LAUGHS]

00:08:10.044 --> 00:08:14.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.830
Some seem to have the impression
that it was ever thus,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.770
that Jane Austen
adaptations have come out

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.100
in a continuous stream since
time immemorial, not at all.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:25.950
How did Jane Austen finally
become film material?

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:29.370
Sense and Sensibility,
her first published book,

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.380
came out in 1811.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.880
The MGM Pride and Prejudice
did not come out--

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.330
which I think was
screened on a Sunday,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.340
did not come out until 1940.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:39.600
It was not badly received.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.669
The New York Times
reviewed it favorably.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:44.250
But it lost money.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:48.990
There would not be another major
film of Jane Austen's works

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.980
for 55 years.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:54.470
You lose money, you
don't make films.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:09:00.300
Jane Austen was in
the film doghouse.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.420
1995 was the year of the
Jane Austen Big Bang,

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.680
four projects coming out, all
successful in their own terms.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.530
When it rains, it pours.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.690
There was Sense and Sensibility,
I consider the best film

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.770
adaptation.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.770
The Colin Firth television,
Pride and Prejudice version,

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:19.290
the best Pride and
Prejudice version probably.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:23.010
There is the British
TV film of Persuasion,

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.510
which many people like.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:27.480
It's kind of a dowdy version
of a beautiful novel,

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.460
but considered successful.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:33.310
And in its own terms,
Clueless can be mentioned.

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:35.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.733
A lot of us hate it, but
no, we don't hate it.

00:09:38.733 --> 00:09:40.150
I just didn't like
it at the time.

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.640
The kind of cool take was,
oh, Clueless is the best Jane

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.750
Austen adaptation.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:46.390
And that turned us against it.

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:50.350
The next year brought the
Gwyneth Paltrow, Emma, the Kate

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:53.290
Beckinsale mini series, Emma,
and the first of the Bridget

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.110
Jones' novels, so heavens.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.370
The floodgates were open.

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:00.820
Jane Austen was material, a go
to source for hacks everywhere,

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.590
and some non hacks.

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.540
The dirty secret of
Jane Austen world;

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:08.330
generally Austen enthusiasts
divided into two groups.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:10.270
This is a dirty
secret, those who

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.210
come to Austen from books and
those who mostly approach her

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.363
from the film and
television adaptations.

00:10:15.363 --> 00:10:17.530
This week you've heard a
lot from those who approach

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:18.760
Austen from her novels.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:21.970
And they're very critical
of a lot of the films.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.520
In the novels, moral
and character insights

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.230
and subtle comedy loom large.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.810
And a lot of those
things are lost in films.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:32.590
Austen film and television fans
are more oriented to romance

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:34.410
and Darcy's in wet shirts.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:35.035
I'm prejudiced.

00:10:35.035 --> 00:10:40.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:41.960
But thanks to film
and TV Austen fans,

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.700
her works have become a genre.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.740
Perhaps Georgette--
Heyer or Hayer?

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:47.600
Hayer, Heyer?

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:51.500
Georgette Heyer, Barbara
Cartland, and Brigerton

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:54.260
are the next tendencies.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:57.200
Jane Austen, in her writing
is mostly anti-romantic.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:00.350
She will give her public the
final outcome they require.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:02.690
But it is quite reserved,
judgmental, thorny path

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:03.590
to get there.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:05.810
She was even more caustic
in her private letters

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.337
and her private perceptions.

00:11:08.337 --> 00:11:09.920
So there are different
ways of looking

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.020
at in judging film adaptations.

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:15.680
This week you've heard a lot of
judging the specific fidelity

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:17.420
of the films to the novels.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:18.890
There's another
school of thought

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:23.180
that a film has no obligation to
be faithful to source material.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:26.200
The only question is, is the
film a good film in itself

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.910
however unfaithful it might be?

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:32.840
There are a couple of theories
about adapting books into film.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:36.500
Though perhaps not directly
relevant to Austen films.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.600
Alexander Payne is
an indie director,

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:43.040
who did a very good film based
on the novel, The Descendents,

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:46.610
about the dramas in the life
of the dynastic Hollywood

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:48.382
Hawaiian family.

00:11:48.382 --> 00:11:49.715
Has anyone seen The Descendents?

00:11:49.715 --> 00:11:51.360
AUDIENCE: Woo!

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.120
WHIT STILLMAN: Yeah,
it's really good.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.130
He went to USC film school.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.870
You know what USC stands for?

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.420
Does anyone know that?

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:00.770
University of spoiled children.

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:02.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:07.040
Very true of the film
school, especially.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.870
And I think in the
cheating scandal

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:12.930
that was also confirmed, the
college cheating scandal.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:14.430
Do people try to
get into Hillsdale?

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.883
I guess so, OK.

00:12:15.883 --> 00:12:16.383
[LAUGHS]

00:12:16.383 --> 00:12:19.624
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:25.280
OK, USC film school in
Alexander Payne's day,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.950
there's a famous class in
book to film adaptation

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:31.820
in which the professor would
ask the students, what the film

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:33.780
owes the original book.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:36.710
And they had to
shout back, nothing.

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:38.750
The film means
nothing to the book.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:41.060
Payne's view is that you
owe nothing to the novel.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:42.650
But you remain
faithful to the spirit.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:45.710
Well, it's fine for him to say.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:47.300
In Austen's case,
though, I think

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.370
most of the concerns
about faithfulness,

00:12:49.370 --> 00:12:52.340
which could be considered
nitpicking, are justified.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:56.810
Certain transformations are
not just unfaithful to Austen's

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:58.250
perspective and insights.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:12:59.780
They traduce them.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:03.660
One turned a classic Austen tale
into Charlotte Bronte romance.

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.020
Why not go to Charlotte
Bronte directly?

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:10.353
The adaptation of her great
moral novel, Mansfield Park,

00:13:10.353 --> 00:13:12.020
was free with all
sorts of contemporary,

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.530
political, and sexual concerns.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:14.030
Why?

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:15.110
Why bother?

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.315
Why not learn from the past
when we have the chance

00:13:17.315 --> 00:13:19.190
to learn from the past,
rather than lecturing

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:21.800
the past, which can't hear us?

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:27.650
Certain flawed books can improve
with well-chosen infidelity.

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.980
Ian Forrester's greatly
admired novel, Howards End,

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:35.090
I didn't admire, I have to say.

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:37.750
I found it manipulative
and tendentious,

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:40.560
one family absolutely charming,
sensitive, and wonderful,

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.368
the other terribly,
terribly bad,

00:13:42.368 --> 00:13:44.410
and probably Republican,
except there in England.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:46.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:51.760
For the film, the director,
James Ivory, screenwriter,

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.545
Ruth Prawer Jhabvala, and
the cast of Anthony Hopkins--

00:13:55.545 --> 00:13:57.670
Emma Thompson is getting
a lot of credit this week,

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.598
Emma Thompson, Vanessa Redgrave
transformed Howards End

00:14:00.598 --> 00:14:02.890
into something really wonderful,
subtle, and beautiful,

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:05.140
and much fairer to
the different people,

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:08.950
although the bad people
were still pretty bad.

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:11.770
When it comes to Hollywood's
search for material, what's

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:16.270
in and what's out, James
Bond and Jane Austen are in.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.390
Charles Dickens and
Anthony Trollope are out.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:22.450
Many of the dynamics of social
tension and moral observation,

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:24.910
people credit for
Austen's popularity,

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.220
would also apply
well to Trollope.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.710
He also has a
reformist perspective

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:31.930
some people reproach
Austen for lacking.

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:35.530
And there are 47 of the
Trollope novels, not just six.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.140
So maybe we can hope and
look forward to that.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:39.970
I recall a meeting
a decade or more

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.520
ago with a very, nice producer.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:45.670
Someone had sent her a
project about werewolves.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:47.980
She was excoriating.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:51.010
Everyone knows it's
vampires people want to see.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:53.530
Young people today
only want vampires.

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.270
How can these people
be so clueless

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:57.040
as to propose a
werewolf project?

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:58.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.648
Little did she suspect that
zombies, of all things,

00:15:01.648 --> 00:15:02.815
were just around the corner.

00:15:02.815 --> 00:15:04.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:08.800
Werewolves still haven't
happened, I don't think.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.240
But there's always hope.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.370
And I think there's
a popular series that

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:14.030
is a Wolverine character.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.040
And so, maybe that's
getting close.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:17.830
OK, adapting Austen.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:20.560
So Jane Austen, like
James Bond, is material.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.750
I experienced this
myself trying to write

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:24.760
my first film, Metropolitan.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:26.770
I needed things for people
to say to each other,

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:30.340
to argue about, defining their
characters and relationships.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:32.140
The idea that the heroine--

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:34.870
called Audrey,
because she was also

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:39.580
inspired by Audrey Hepburn,
was a kind of Fanny Price.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:41.500
This first Jane
Austen collaboration

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:44.440
was slight and nearly
accidental beginning,

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.870
as do many things, with
a misunderstanding.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.090
I was praising
Austen's Mansfield Park

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.640
to a literary minded
friend, when he informed me

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:54.550
that the literary critic, Lionel
Trilling, whom we both admired,

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.220
had destroyed Mansfield
Park, particularly

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.980
its first, virtuous
heroine, Fanny Price.

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:03.010
Trilling had written,
"Nobody, I believe,

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:04.960
has ever found it
possible to like

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:07.270
the heroine of Mansfield Park.

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:11.510
Fanny Price is overtly virtuous
and consciously virtuous.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:16.310
Our modern, literary feeling
is very strong against people

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.740
who, when they mean
to be virtuous,

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:22.310
believe they know how to reach
their goal and do reach it.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:25.070
We think that virtue
is not interesting."

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.105
Don't come to Hillsdale.

00:16:26.105 --> 00:16:26.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:32.420
"Even that is not really virtue,
unless it manifests itself

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:36.500
as a product of grace operating
through a strong inclination

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:37.680
to sin."

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.480
Wow.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.510
Our favorite Saint is
likely to be Augustine.

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:47.100
At this point, we will
pause for film clips.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.610
So I hope it works.

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:51.550
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:52.383
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:16:52.383 --> 00:16:56.290
- By Tolstoy, War and Peace,
and by Jane Austen, Persuasion

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:57.370
and Mansfield Park.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:00.110
- Mansfield Park, you've
got to be kidding.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:00.760
- No.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.380
- But it's a
notoriously bad book,

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:05.470
even Lionel Trilling, one of her
greatest admirers thought that.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.829
- Well if Lionel Trilling
thought that, he's an idiot.

00:17:08.829 --> 00:17:10.660
- The whole story
revolves around what,

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:13.540
the immorality of a group of
young people putting on a play.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:16.652
- In the context of a novel
it makes perfect sense.

00:17:16.652 --> 00:17:18.819
- But the context of the
novel and nearly everything

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:20.861
Jane Austen wrote is near
ridiculous from today's

00:17:20.861 --> 00:17:21.982
perspective.

00:17:21.982 --> 00:17:23.690
- Has it ever occurred
to you that today,

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:25.420
looked at from Jane
Austen's perspective,

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:26.349
would look even worse?

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:29.840
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:34.652
WHIT STILLMAN: No, let's
do the next, lights.

00:17:34.652 --> 00:17:35.510
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:38.420
- I read that Lionel
Trilling essay you mentioned.

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:39.830
You really like Trilling?

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:41.610
- Yes.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.020
- I think he's very strange.

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:47.760
He says that nobody could like
the heroine of Mansfield Park.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.440
I like her.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.150
And he goes on and
on about how we

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:53.760
modern people of today
with our modern attitudes

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:57.270
bitterly resent Mansfield
Park because its heroine is

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:58.830
virtuous.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:01.230
What's wrong with a novel
having a virtuous heroine?

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:02.910
- His point is that the
novel's premise, that there's

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:05.535
something immoral in a group of
young people putting on a play,

00:18:05.535 --> 00:18:07.080
is simply absurd.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:08.890
- You found Fanny
Price unlikable?

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.730
- She sounds pretty unbearable,
but I haven't read the book.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:12.505
- What?

00:18:12.505 --> 00:18:14.880
- You don't have to read a
book to have an opinion on it.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.340
I've haven't read
the Bible either.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.620
- What Jane Austen
novels have you read?

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:20.310
- None.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:21.420
I don't read novels.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.128
I prefer a good
literary criticism.

00:18:23.128 --> 00:18:25.170
That way you get both the
novelists' idea as well

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:26.490
as the critics' thinking.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:28.170
With fiction I can never
forget that none of it

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:30.570
ever really happened, that it's
all just made up by the author.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:31.153
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:31.153 --> 00:18:32.007
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:32.007 --> 00:18:33.444
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:37.550
WHIT STILLMAN: Thank you.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:44.360
So Jane Austen threw me a
lifeline for this movie.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:48.950
Trilling, in his essay,
is incredulous that one

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:50.450
of the novel's key
controversies--

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:52.400
and you've been over this so
many times I hate to bring it

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:52.970
up--

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.340
when Fanny Price's Bertram
cousins and their guests

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:57.410
propose to a play at
home, and she objects,

00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:01.730
setting her absent uncle and
benefactors opposition to them.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:03.770
I mean, I think a lot
of the descriptions

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:08.240
of Fanny's objections
to the theatricals

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:10.740
are off in the sense that
she's not against theatricals.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.320
She's not just
against theatricals.

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:17.060
It's the specific situation
with her uncle, who's

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:21.860
laid down the law against
this happening very firmly,

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:23.370
and he's taken her in.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.520
And she came from such
a horrible background

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:30.230
in Portsmouth, and he generously
has treated her as a daughter.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:31.670
And he's gone.

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:36.740
And her elder cousin,
the Miss Bertram,

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:39.510
has a very fragile engagement.

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:41.240
It's sort of in jeopardy.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:44.870
And the idea of the
danger of this play

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:49.880
in her fragile situation--
so I think people sort of

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:52.370
are unfair to Jane
Austen and Fanny Price

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:54.020
in how this is all happening.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.390
But what was sort
of a lifeline for me

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:58.370
in getting this
material-- it's very hard

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:01.070
sort of to come up with
a plot and situations

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:03.690
that reveal characters
and advance the story.

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.690
And so, I started thinking,
what could be liked,

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:09.440
this sort of indefensible
position that someone takes,

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:12.080
where it isolates them
from everyone else.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:14.540
Like someone, who does
not want to do something,

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:17.150
everyone else wants to do
it, and their objections

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:18.680
seem stupid, and silly.

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:20.450
And they're idiots,
and why do you--

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:21.590
why do you want to do this?

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:24.540
And this happens a lot.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:30.870
And I remembered a case
shortly after college

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:34.050
where there was one of
those truth or dare games.

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:36.660
And I didn't like
the idea very much.

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:37.710
I didn't object to it.

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.040
And it was really terrible.

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:40.500
I mean, some people
ask questions

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.420
they shouldn't have
asked, and the people

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.795
felt obliged to answer when
they shouldn't have answered.

00:20:44.795 --> 00:20:47.520
And it was really embarrassing
and bad, bad, bad, bad.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:51.000
And so, I thought
maybe someone could--

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.600
sort of a more frivolous
type, could propose a truth

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.760
or dare session.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:02.040
And Audrey objects,
and she's isolated.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:06.330
And then something comes out
in that that's sad and bad.

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:07.890
And it advances the plot.

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:09.780
So anyway, it really
helped me out.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:12.180
I mean, it's small
in the whole movie.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.400
But the Jane Austen
element was helpful.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:21.610
Also, as an aside, one thing
I've been thrilled here.

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:23.860
I normally am
blocked from JSTOR.

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:25.350
Do you know what JSTOR is?

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:29.013
All the-- normally I have
to, you know, do some log in.

00:21:29.013 --> 00:21:30.930
And I can look at it,
but I can't download it.

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:32.210
I'm such a cheapskate.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:33.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:35.070
And there are just so many
things that I want to research.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:35.903
And I come to JSTOR.

00:21:35.903 --> 00:21:37.950
And it's, OK, now I can
read it, OK, quickly.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:39.030
I can't download it.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:42.240
And then magically, I
was here, in my room,

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:43.115
and I'd go to things.

00:21:43.115 --> 00:21:44.115
And I could download it.

00:21:44.115 --> 00:21:45.020
It said download.

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:46.560
Download, OK, download.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:51.853
So tomorrow morning, I'm going
to be downloading every article

00:21:51.853 --> 00:21:52.770
I ever wanted to read.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:56.043
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:56.043 --> 00:21:56.918
Thank you, Hillsdale.

00:21:56.918 --> 00:21:59.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:59.310 --> 00:22:01.690
So anyway, Metropolitan
even got to be

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:05.050
considered a stealth
adaptation of Mansfield Park

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:05.740
by some people.

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:07.930
So, it got Jane Austen
talked about a little bit.

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:14.320
Oh, and about these essays,
the Lionel Trilling essay

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:15.260
is really great.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.640
It's a really fascinating essay.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:21.760
He published it in the
Partisan Review in 1954

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:24.550
and collected it in his
book, The Opposing Self.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:27.640
And it's also just available
free on the internet.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.220
I guess it's been pirated.

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:36.640
And then, there is a
wonderful essay replying to it

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:40.570
from a retired Smith
College Professor, who

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:42.620
recently died at 100 years old.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:45.280
So we don't know if he saw it.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:47.920
His name is Paul Pickrell.

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:51.340
And he wrote this essay, Lionel
Trilling and Mansfield Park.

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:52.255
It's on JSTOR.

00:22:52.255 --> 00:22:53.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:55.060
And it's really interesting.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:59.710
He destroys
Trilling, but nicely.

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:02.710
So my second Jane Austen
adaptation learning experience

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:05.330
came--

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.650
I was really lucky
with Metropolitan.

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:13.630
I was completely accessible,
and happy to meet with anyone,

00:23:13.630 --> 00:23:15.070
and do any project.

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:18.830
In fact there's sort of a set
thing with indie filmmakers--

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:20.680
oh, my God, is it
getting that late?

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:22.630
I better hurry up.

00:23:22.630 --> 00:23:26.740
So, that you make a first film,
and then studios come to call.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:29.393
And you get to make James Bond
films or interesting films.

00:23:29.393 --> 00:23:31.060
You don't have to
make your indie films.

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:32.950
But, I want to make
a second indie film,

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:34.330
which is a real mistake
because then they think,

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:36.640
oh, you're only going to
do these little films.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:38.270
They can draw a line
between the two.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:40.437
Anyway, I was in the editing
room for a second film,

00:23:40.437 --> 00:23:43.900
Barcelona, and I already had
the idea for the third film,

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:45.460
The Last Days of Disco.

00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:48.655
And a producer came out.

00:23:48.655 --> 00:23:50.530
I was working with this
company, Castle Rock,

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:52.603
which Rob Reiner started.

00:23:52.603 --> 00:23:53.770
And it's this great company.

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:55.187
And they backed
three of my films.

00:23:55.187 --> 00:23:56.680
So I'm really grateful to them.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:58.780
And this wonderful
producer, Lindsay Duran,

00:23:58.780 --> 00:23:59.950
had worked there.

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:02.647
And she wanted to adapt
Sense and Sensibility.

00:24:02.647 --> 00:24:04.480
And she went first to
Rob Reiner because she

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:06.855
thought there should be in
character, comedy guy who does

00:24:06.855 --> 00:24:08.440
this, character comedy guy.

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:09.670
Rob Reiner passed on it.

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:10.990
She came to me.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:14.848
I had this idea that I
should write original stories

00:24:14.848 --> 00:24:15.640
and then film them.

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:17.307
And I was in between
these two projects.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:20.675
But I read the script.

00:24:20.675 --> 00:24:22.300
And it was Emma
Thompson's, early draft

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:23.620
of Emma Thompson's script.

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:25.750
And it started just
with the dialogue scene.

00:24:25.750 --> 00:24:27.760
So she took the
novel, got to the part

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.460
where they're dialogue,
and dramatized them.

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:33.550
And it seemed very drawing
room and sort of not

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:34.840
dramatic and not passionate.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:37.060
So I read the novel,
and I realized

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:39.430
there's a very, brief
narration at the beginning,

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:40.780
without dialogue.

00:24:40.780 --> 00:24:44.410
And that it gives the whole
predicament of these sisters,

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:48.780
who are the second family of--

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:51.280
I mean, if you've seen the film,
so you all know the story--

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:57.160
and how the sister-in-law
talks their elder brother away

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:00.310
from the promise he gave their
father to take care of them.

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:02.470
So they're left
essentially, destitute,

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:04.090
in a horrible predicament.

00:25:04.090 --> 00:25:07.825
And Lindsay Duran said,
"Yeah, that sort of--

00:25:07.825 --> 00:25:08.950
I was thinking about that."

00:25:08.950 --> 00:25:11.652
And wonderful director came
on to do that, Ang Lee,

00:25:11.652 --> 00:25:13.360
with a writing
collaborator, Jim Sheamus.

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:14.908
They worked with Emma Thompson.

00:25:14.908 --> 00:25:16.450
That film turned
out so well I think,

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:18.950
because you had these really,
brilliant filmmakers, and then

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:21.460
someone who really loved Austen
and is really close to it,

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:23.950
and knew the milieu,
Emma Thompson.

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:26.740
And that collaboration
was so wonderful.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:29.620
And it really taught me to learn
that you can't really judge

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:31.360
a script by an early draft.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:33.122
It's very hard to
imagine what a film's

00:25:33.122 --> 00:25:35.455
going to be like from a script,
all the good things that

00:25:35.455 --> 00:25:37.990
are going to be done,
and changed, and adapted.

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:44.530
And also, that certain authors
have wonderful predicaments.

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:47.050
And one of the things
that makes Jane Austen so

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:49.990
great to adapt normally is
she has great predicaments.

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:54.130
I mean, they're always so
interesting and sort of subtle.

00:25:54.130 --> 00:26:01.460
In any case, what
happened next was I

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:04.370
did that film, The
Last Days of Disco.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:05.610
It wasn't profitable.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.170
So, Hollywood wasn't
calling immediately.

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:11.270
It was well received
but not profitable.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:17.330
And I had also felt that the
three, dramatic stories I

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:18.590
had to tell, I'd kind of done.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:21.770
And I had to have a
dramatic experience before I

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:22.770
could do something else.

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:24.755
And so, I was thinking
of doing adaptations.

00:26:24.755 --> 00:26:26.130
I got involved
with other people.

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:27.830
And they all kind of went wrong.

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:29.840
And there were all these
books under copyright,

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:33.110
with living authors, and
studios, and producers.

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:36.360
And like, you get four people,
five people involved in it

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:39.110
everyone, likes something
different in the novel.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:40.550
Everyone has a different idea--

00:26:40.550 --> 00:26:42.740
or the memoir or
whatever you're adapting.

00:26:42.740 --> 00:26:45.787
And then, there's a lot of money
for each year of the option.

00:26:45.787 --> 00:26:47.120
You haven't finished the script.

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:48.350
It's not good enough,
and they don't

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:49.620
want to pay for next
year, the option.

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:51.330
They don't want to
buy it out, outright.

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:52.538
There are all these problems.

00:26:52.538 --> 00:26:54.950
And living authors are big
problems, let me tell you.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:55.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:58.190
Because living authors
very often want to get

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:00.410
into the sausage factory
just when they shouldn't.

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:03.230
Because things are pretty
ugly in the sausage factory.

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:07.490
And so they sneakily get, bad,
early drafts, and read them,

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:08.740
and say, oh, this is terrible.

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:11.480
[LAUGHTER] And
then they really do

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:13.720
everything possible to
torpedo the project.

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:19.100
So, Jane Austen also has the
great virtue of never wanting

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:20.000
to read your script--

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:21.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:23.910
--of never getting
involved in any way.

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:27.455
And if you can keep it
secret, you don't have to pay.

00:27:27.455 --> 00:27:29.762
You know. it's public domain.

00:27:29.762 --> 00:27:30.720
So that's a good thing.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:33.470
So I was reading
Jane Austen again.

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:37.285
And it's because I stupidly
read Northanger Abbey first

00:27:37.285 --> 00:27:38.660
when I was a
sophomore in college

00:27:38.660 --> 00:27:40.700
and hated it, and would tell
everyone, I hate Jane Austen.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:41.720
She's overrated.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:43.670
She's ter-- how can
people like her?

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:45.200
How can they like her?

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:47.867
And then my smart,
elder sister convinced

00:27:47.867 --> 00:27:48.950
me to read something else.

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:50.340
So I read Sense and
Sensibility, and loved it,

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:51.590
and read everything
else, and loved it.

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:53.570
And when I was writing
Metropolitan, just

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:59.065
sort of pause from the
sausage factory of trying

00:27:59.065 --> 00:28:00.440
to write the
script, I'd actually

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:03.350
just read pages of Pride and
Prejudice to clear the palette,

00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:04.950
or cleanse the
brain, or whatever.

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.370
So I was reading this
omnibus, Jane Austen.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:10.190
And I ran into all these
strange things, all

00:28:10.190 --> 00:28:12.380
these strange, little
things I didn't know about.

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:15.890
So I got a big, big Jane
Austen idea and then

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:20.510
a trivial, pathetic, sad,
ridiculous Jane Austen idea.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:23.502
And the big, big, cool
Jane Austen idea, I

00:28:23.502 --> 00:28:25.460
immediately got a producer
who wanted to do it.

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:28.580
I took it to one of the
big commercial studios

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:32.090
in England that does all
big, romantic, you know,

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:34.910
Bridget Jones, Notting
Hill, all these big things.

00:28:34.910 --> 00:28:35.750
And I went to them.

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:38.780
And they said, Oh, we don't like
to do anything but the biggest

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:39.680
novel of an author.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:41.420
We'll only do the biggest novel.

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:42.710
Guess what novel they did?

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:44.250
Anyway.

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:47.300
And they did they did two
of those, both, anyway.

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:49.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:53.840
So, I had this very slight
idea, this strange manuscript,

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:57.950
Jane Austen, left untitled,
about widowed, Lady Susan,

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:00.020
De Courcy, and her quest
for wealthy husband

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:02.280
for herself and her daughter.

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:03.980
It was in the epistolary style.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:06.020
It was really hard
to read through.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:08.900
There's like a perfunctory
wrapping up at the end, not

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:11.420
a real conclusion.

00:29:11.420 --> 00:29:16.010
But what gave me hope was that
I knew two British actresses who

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:19.880
were beautiful and brilliant at
comedy, Elizabeth Hurley, who

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:22.422
through Hugh Grant was sort of
part of the Castle Rock Group.

00:29:22.422 --> 00:29:24.797
And I knew she she was a really
smart producer and really

00:29:24.797 --> 00:29:25.430
clever.

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:29.480
And so, Elizabeth Hurley was
the right age then for the part.

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:34.040
And also, she was at the top of
her Austin Powers popularity.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:35.900
There was one time--

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:38.750
there's one point in time
when Elizabeth Hurley was

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:40.940
the favorite actress
of 14-year-old boys--

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:42.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:44.450
--which means a lot
in the film business.

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:46.154
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:46.154 --> 00:29:50.060
And Kate Beckinsale,
Kate Beckinsale, I knew

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:51.470
would be great as Lady Susan.

00:29:51.470 --> 00:29:53.030
But she was too young then.

00:29:53.030 --> 00:29:56.540
Because this was like 10 years
before I got the script ready.

00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:59.930
And from The Last Days of
Disco, she went on to make--

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:03.410
to become a Gothic,
action-film star.

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:07.280
And anyway, but the idea
that an actress can do it

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:09.720
makes a thing more interesting.

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:13.370
So I'm curiously--
I was in London

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:15.920
and happen to have cocktails
with a group of young people.

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:20.150
And a writer for City
Journal and her boyfriend,

00:30:20.150 --> 00:30:26.420
was a really nice theatrical
producer, very small scale,

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.280
running a tiny
theatre in London,

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:31.070
he was sort of a
Scottish, awkward,

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:32.690
nicer version of Hugh Grant.

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:33.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:40.678
And I told him this idea, this
strange idea, of Lady Susan.

00:30:40.678 --> 00:30:41.720
And he said he'd read it.

00:30:41.720 --> 00:30:42.770
And he read it,
and he thought you

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:44.062
know this could be interesting.

00:30:44.062 --> 00:30:48.180
So I said why not do it as a
little play to test it out,

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:49.710
try out the material.

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:51.360
And then we could
make it maybe--

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:54.080
make it definitely into a movie
later, that would be the plan.

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:56.300
And so he gave me like
a tiny amount of money

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:59.920
that I could well afford to pay
back, a very loose contract,

00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:02.120
and I started working on it.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:05.230
And adapting this
with the letters

00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:10.390
you sort of take the letters
from Alicia to Lady Susan,

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:12.190
and put several
letters together,

00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:14.620
and try to make it a scene,
a conversational scene.

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:17.617
You've got these essentially,
monologue, and monologue,

00:31:17.617 --> 00:31:19.450
and monologue, and
monologue, and monologue.

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:21.470
And you tried to get it to work.

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:23.680
And then you had to
De Courcey's, trying

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:26.140
to deal with this shark
coming into their world

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:29.210
and their letters to each
other, and you sort of

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:30.610
start getting something.

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:33.910
And what was strange is that
normally a script, before you

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:35.920
finished it-- even
when you finished it,

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:37.720
is just terrible
for people to read.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:40.030
Like normal people cannot
enjoy a film script.

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.370
It's broken up in the
interior, exterior, and night,

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:43.900
day, all the nonsense.

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:46.210
And in this case it
was really strange

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:49.150
because I was really far away
from finishing the script.

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:50.470
But it's really fun to read.

00:31:50.470 --> 00:31:52.053
Because a lot of the
stuff I had to do

00:31:52.053 --> 00:31:54.490
is cut out funny
Jane Austen stuff.

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:55.990
But it was there,
and so my daughter

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:56.830
could read it and like it.

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:58.300
And other friends could like it.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:01.582
And I tweeted that I was
thinking of doing a novel.

00:32:01.582 --> 00:32:03.540
And someone actually,
miraculously tweeted back

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:05.490
saying they'd like to
see it, a publisher.

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:07.410
And I sent them
this script I had.

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:08.610
I hadn't finished.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:10.850
And they loved it and
said, oh, we'll do a novel.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:18.390
And so I had a contract to do
the derivative, knock-off novel

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:22.650
before I had any backing for
the film or a finished script.

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:25.230
And Trevor, the
play producer, he

00:32:25.230 --> 00:32:27.450
would always be saying,
well, what about Frederica?

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:28.325
What about Frederica?

00:32:28.325 --> 00:32:30.700
Because there wasn't much
about Frederica in the letters,

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:33.300
and he thought that Frederica
had to be the heart of the film

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:34.675
and had to be the
heart and soul.

00:32:34.675 --> 00:32:36.930
And I couldn't think of
anything for Frederica really.

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:38.040
What about Frederica?

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:40.570
And anyway, in the novel,
if you ever read it,

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:42.713
there's a lot, what
about Frederica?

00:32:42.713 --> 00:32:44.880
And we have a lot more about
Frederica in the novel.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:51.340
Anyway, so, Trevor went on to
do natural-resources investing.

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:56.500
[LAUGHS] And I decided that I
hated the theater, play format

00:32:56.500 --> 00:32:58.798
because cinema is so brilliant.

00:32:58.798 --> 00:33:00.340
And I hate when
people talk about how

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:02.800
a great, 10-minute shot--
because the great thing

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.990
in cinema is to be able
to jump from one place

00:33:04.990 --> 00:33:07.870
to another, to cut, to get
closer, to get farther away,

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:10.390
to do all these things that
are so wonderful in cinema.

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:11.690
And theater doesn't have that.

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:14.620
And I do want to do plays
and things like that someday.

00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:16.940
But in this case, it
was very frustrating,

00:33:16.940 --> 00:33:19.600
the idea of adapting it for a
play to later do a movie, no.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:20.870
Why not do the movie directly?

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:22.745
So I started doing the
movie script directly.

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:32.595
Anyway, there was
one, key letter.

00:33:32.595 --> 00:33:34.560
And it was Catherine Vernon.

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:38.460
And Austen scholars
write about this letter.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:40.920
Because they say this is
the letter where she's

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:42.280
fighting the epistolary style.

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:46.570
She's trying to write
modern, novel scenes.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:47.970
So it's a very, long letter.

00:33:47.970 --> 00:33:52.050
And in it, Catherine Vernon
describes this silly, city man,

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:54.960
Sir James Martin, coming to.

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:57.690
And Lady Susan's manipulating
him to marry Frederica.

00:33:57.690 --> 00:33:58.992
And Frederica doesn't want to.

00:33:58.992 --> 00:34:00.450
And he just laughs,
laughs, laughs.

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:03.690
[IMITATES LAUGHING SOUND] And it
was all about how silly he was.

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:05.250
But there was
actually no matter.

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:06.870
There was actually no scene.

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:09.870
And so, it was very hard to
try to come up with something.

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.110
And I came up with something.

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:13.320
And it was pretty lame.

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:16.664
And in auditions, I just got
so sick of this lame scene

00:34:16.664 --> 00:34:18.360
that I wrote another scene.

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:19.739
And it's a little better.

00:34:19.739 --> 00:34:22.050
But it's still pretty
lame, you know,

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:25.830
Church and Hill, Churchhill,
and all, you know,

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:27.690
lame, lame stuff.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:29.558
Then a guy comes in
to audition for it.

00:34:29.558 --> 00:34:32.100
There are actually three guys
who came in to audition for it,

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:33.267
and they're all pretty good.

00:34:33.267 --> 00:34:34.530
One guy was kind of a genius.

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:36.810
And he was so funny
with this lame scene.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.170
I couldn't believe it.

00:34:38.170 --> 00:34:41.100
And we finally got
the-- oh, there's

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:42.810
another thing that
happened that will

00:34:42.810 --> 00:34:45.300
interest the
investors, upstairs,

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:46.770
and in the restaurant.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:48.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:53.460
A Christian investor wanted to
back my Jamaican Church film.

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:54.870
But I wasn't ready to do that.

00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:56.963
I was trying to do
this Jane Austen film.

00:34:56.963 --> 00:34:58.380
But he had his son
working for him

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:01.530
who said that my script
didn't have Christian mission.

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:05.060
And I said, OK, what can I do?

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:06.630
How do I get the
Christianity in it?

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:07.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:10.400
So, I thought Commandments,
honor their father and mother.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:13.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:17.500
Honor their father and mother
is my favorite commandment

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:18.490
because I'm a father.

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:19.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:22.540
And I used to have one.

00:35:22.540 --> 00:35:26.350
I think sort of all
philosophy can come out

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:28.465
of that which I
mistakenly thought

00:35:28.465 --> 00:35:29.590
was the fourth commandment.

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:30.090
[LAUGHS]

00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:32.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:32.970 --> 00:35:35.265
Anyway, meanwhile, the
other important commandment

00:35:35.265 --> 00:35:39.408
is the ninth commandment, which
we really need these days.

00:35:39.408 --> 00:35:40.950
And so, it was
putting a lot of this.

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:42.810
And now I had Frederica
scenes because they could

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:43.977
talk about the Commandments.

00:35:43.977 --> 00:35:46.210
And Federico's coming into it.

00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:48.730
And then I said, well, I've
always wanted to do a sermon.

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:50.230
I want to do a
sermon on aesthetics.

00:35:50.230 --> 00:35:52.230
So we'll have a
church and a curate.

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:54.880
And Frederica can go
to pray in the church.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:57.120
And the curate can come
and give his little sermon

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:59.850
and talk about the fourth
and fifth Commandments.

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:03.930
And so, suddenly all this
new material is coming in.

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:09.690
And this story that was just
sort of locked into this bitchy

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:15.270
dialogues between
the two characters,

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.610
suddenly it started
broadening and become bigger.

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:23.190
And then a real Eureka moment
was in the terrible read

00:36:23.190 --> 00:36:23.690
through.

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:25.540
So, before you shoot
a film, you do a read

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:28.522
through of all the
actors you have on set.

00:36:28.522 --> 00:36:30.730
And we're going through,
and it's just, just painful.

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:32.350
The material
doesn't sound great.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:38.320
And we just had this
laptop just sitting there.

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:42.250
And at one point the
laptop started acting.

00:36:42.250 --> 00:36:43.740
And it was Sir James Martin.

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:45.150
And he was incredibly funny.

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:49.110
And he was Skyping in
from Brighton, England.

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:50.280
We were in Dublin.

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:51.600
And he was hilarious.

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:55.140
And suddenly this disastrous
read through didn't seem bad.

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:57.390
And I started thinking,
I've got to write a lot more

00:36:57.390 --> 00:36:59.250
material for this guy.

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:03.360
And also-- I mean
Chloe Sevigny had

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:06.310
come with a beautiful, English
accent she'd worked hard on.

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:07.380
It was beautiful.

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:10.025
But I'd been through
three films where

00:37:10.025 --> 00:37:11.400
there were accent
problems, where

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:12.525
you worry about the accent.

00:37:12.525 --> 00:37:14.130
No matter how good
it is, there's

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:16.447
someone who will say--
you know, they'll

00:37:16.447 --> 00:37:18.030
know the actor is
not from that place.

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:18.905
And they'll say that.

00:37:18.905 --> 00:37:21.990
So we went through this with
a fake accent in Damsels

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:22.830
in Distress.

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:24.877
A woman consciously
had a fake accent.

00:37:24.877 --> 00:37:26.460
And still people
would see the trailer

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.230
and would say, oh, it was
a fake accent in this film.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:29.430
Yeah, there is a fake accent.

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:30.138
It's intentional.

00:37:30.138 --> 00:37:31.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:33.390
So, I just hated that accent.

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:35.670
And the assistant
director, who is English,

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:41.460
he took me aside for a pint
of stout Guinness in Dublin.

00:37:41.460 --> 00:37:45.210
It was the best
paint I've ever had.

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:49.020
Because he said why not
make Alicia American?

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:51.300
And I was in the
midst of just reading

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:55.680
about the War of Independence
and the poor Tories,

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:58.350
who were in exile to Nova
Scotia and back to London.

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:01.020
And there are these American
families, the Delanceys

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:02.400
who are just so English.

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:03.390
They went to Eaton.

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:05.340
And they read law at
the Inns of Court.

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:06.702
And they were just so English.

00:38:06.702 --> 00:38:08.160
And they came to
the United States.

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:09.600
And they were thrown out,
as they should have been.

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:10.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:13.302
And they went back to
London and struggled.

00:38:13.302 --> 00:38:14.760
And there's some
beautiful artists,

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:18.360
Benjamin West did a beautiful,
a beautiful painting

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:23.040
of Britannia, welcoming
the loyalists back.

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:31.092
And it's just striking, striking
painting, the ingrate, traitor.

00:38:31.092 --> 00:38:31.592
[LAUGHS]

00:38:31.592 --> 00:38:32.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:36.682
And anyway, so I had all this
material I knew from my film--

00:38:36.682 --> 00:38:37.390
I'm about to end.

00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:38.530
I better end.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:42.490
I knew from my film,
Barcelona, that people

00:38:42.490 --> 00:38:48.250
both in the United States
and Europe love characters

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:49.240
snobbing Americans.

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:50.392
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:50.392 --> 00:38:51.600
Everyone thinks that's funny.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:52.750
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:54.762
And so, having all these
English people making

00:38:54.762 --> 00:38:56.470
comments about her
being American, right?

00:38:56.470 --> 00:38:58.450
I knew that this is comic gold.

00:38:58.450 --> 00:39:00.260
And instead of being exiled--

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:04.060
so in the Jane Austen, she's--
the threat Mr. Johnson gives,

00:39:04.060 --> 00:39:07.940
is to be exiled to
an English village.

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:10.610
In this case she'll be
exiled to Connecticut,

00:39:10.610 --> 00:39:11.725
to Hartford, Connecticut.

00:39:11.725 --> 00:39:12.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:12.320 --> 00:39:15.680
And I knew Connecticut's
going to be a big laugh line.

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:19.940
And anyway, I'd better
wind down, I guess.

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:20.960
I could keep talking.

00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:22.800
But I think they want--

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:23.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:24.050
Anyway, thank you.

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:27.345
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:27.345 --> 00:39:27.845
Thank you.

00:39:27.845 --> 00:39:29.952
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:29.952 --> 00:39:31.410
MODERATOR: Thank
you, Mr. Stillman.

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:33.800
We now have some
time for questions.

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:36.207
So if you have a question,
please come to a microphone.

00:39:44.990 --> 00:39:47.720
AUDIENCE: Hello, first
off, I just want to say,

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:49.910
I really love your work.

00:39:49.910 --> 00:39:52.200
It's such an honor
to hear you talk.

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:54.150
Thank you so much.

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:58.730
One of the things you mentioned
is how most modern Jane Austen

00:39:58.730 --> 00:40:01.010
adaptations focus
on the romance.

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:03.260
And one of the reasons I
think Love and Friendship,

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:07.138
I like it so much, is that it's
very much a comedy of manners.

00:40:07.138 --> 00:40:09.680
Is that part of the reason you
were drawn to it do you think?

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:12.290
And would you consider ever
doing another Jane Austen

00:40:12.290 --> 00:40:13.430
adaptation?

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:15.433
WHIT STILLMAN: Thanks
very much for that.

00:40:15.433 --> 00:40:16.850
There are a lot
of things that are

00:40:16.850 --> 00:40:21.560
really exciting about
realizing how good it was.

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:23.263
So, I'd always had
real reluctance

00:40:23.263 --> 00:40:25.430
of taking one of the great
ones and chopping it down

00:40:25.430 --> 00:40:27.842
to a classic, comic book movie.

00:40:27.842 --> 00:40:30.050
Oh, I know people have done
beautiful jobs with them,

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:31.940
and a beautiful job can be done.

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:33.620
But there are just
so many aspects

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:35.930
of the Lady Susan material.

00:40:35.930 --> 00:40:38.690
It seemed so
unlikely, originally.

00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:40.880
It's really her
funniest material.

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:42.380
It's really funny.

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:44.990
That humor is kind of buried
in this awkward format.

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:47.730
I love the fact that there
is no real title to it.

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:51.170
And I thought I should take
another Austenian title

00:40:51.170 --> 00:40:52.100
and use that.

00:40:52.100 --> 00:40:53.840
And that was probably a mistake.

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:57.320
So I took Love and Friendship
from her earlier juvenilia

00:40:57.320 --> 00:40:58.170
and put it on this.

00:40:58.170 --> 00:41:00.140
But I felt it's authentic
Jane Austen title.

00:41:00.140 --> 00:41:01.970
It didn't have a
Jane Austen title.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:04.700
And I felt there was a
lot of things we could do.

00:41:04.700 --> 00:41:06.600
There's a lot that
had to be added.

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:08.090
It was not subtraction.

00:41:08.090 --> 00:41:09.080
It was additions.

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:13.280
And to conclude the thing
with Sir James Martin--

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:17.870
so we had the conversations
between Lady Susan

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:20.120
and her daughter about
the Commandments.

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:23.870
And in the production
then one afternoon,

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:27.408
the charming, Irish
producer, Katy Hawley,

00:41:27.408 --> 00:41:29.075
happened to mention
the 12 Commandments.

00:41:29.075 --> 00:41:29.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:29.990 --> 00:41:34.250
And I said, [LAUGHS]
Katy, you made the film--

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:36.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:39.680
--through James Martin,
the 12 Commandments.

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:42.230
And then the thing, when
I was thinking about that,

00:41:42.230 --> 00:41:44.580
the idea that--

00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:46.373
someone corrected me
and said it's only 10.

00:41:46.373 --> 00:41:48.290
And I said, there are
two you get to take off.

00:41:48.290 --> 00:41:48.790
[LAUGHS]

00:41:48.790 --> 00:41:50.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:51.940
That was my favorite part.

00:41:51.940 --> 00:41:52.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:56.390
And so, I got in the habit.

00:41:56.390 --> 00:41:59.477
I did something with
a pilot with Amazon

00:41:59.477 --> 00:42:00.560
called, The Cosmopolitans.

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:02.450
And they kept asking
me to write new scenes.

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:03.928
So, Chloe is here.

00:42:03.928 --> 00:42:05.720
Couldn't you have
another scene with Chloe?

00:42:05.720 --> 00:42:08.510
And so I'd write a new scene
like 4 o'clock in the morning.

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:10.640
And so, I realized, you
can write new scenes.

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:12.350
So I kept writing new scenes.

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:13.910
So I wrote that like at four AM.

00:42:13.910 --> 00:42:17.000
And he got it in
the makeup chair.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:19.610
And he kept telling the makeup
people, take a long time,

00:42:19.610 --> 00:42:20.480
take a long time.

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:23.060
And it was just amazing he could
do that scene because he had

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:24.500
so little time with the script.

00:42:24.500 --> 00:42:26.870
And he just took his
time, and did it.

00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:28.880
And whenever he'd
come to set I'd

00:42:28.880 --> 00:42:30.676
try to think of a
new scene for him.

00:42:30.676 --> 00:42:35.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:38.080
AUDIENCE: Hello, I just wanted
to ask how important you

00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:43.558
think it is to be faithful to
a novel and a film adaptation?

00:42:43.558 --> 00:42:45.100
WHIT STILLMAN: Can
I hear that again?

00:42:45.100 --> 00:42:47.100
AUDIENCE: I just wanted
to ask how important you

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:50.530
think it is to be faithful to
a novel in a film adaptation?

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:55.690
WHIT STILLMAN: I mean,
I agree with all sides.

00:42:55.690 --> 00:42:56.950
Is that a courageous stance?

00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:00.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:00.280 --> 00:43:03.010
WHIT STILLMAN: So I think
it's really electrifying,

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:06.740
the university's loyal,
children's version.

00:43:06.740 --> 00:43:08.120
You owe nothing to it.

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:13.610
I mean, the important thing
is that it's a good film.

00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:20.170
And I had a kind of an
argument with a film critic.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:23.020
He was a really good critic,
who was working for a newspaper.

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.960
And he was about to
see a film and was

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:28.600
rushing to read the novel.

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:30.670
And I said to him, oh,
please don't do that.

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:32.800
That's just the worst
thing to do to a movie,

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:34.900
to read the novel right
before you see it.

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:36.430
And so I said--

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:38.260
and I said, why do you do that?

00:43:38.260 --> 00:43:40.180
It's like this homework,
to be a good student

00:43:40.180 --> 00:43:42.220
they have to do that research.

00:43:42.220 --> 00:43:45.050
And he said the editors tell
us that we have to do that.

00:43:45.050 --> 00:43:47.920
We have to know the novel
so we can tell readers

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:49.580
if it's faithful or not.

00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:51.820
So a lot of readers of
the newspapers who've

00:43:51.820 --> 00:43:55.840
read the books want to if
the adaptation is faithful.

00:43:55.840 --> 00:43:58.720
So it's kind of a market
thing I guess, partly.

00:43:58.720 --> 00:44:00.250
And I said why not
read it after--

00:44:00.250 --> 00:44:02.500
I guess they have deadlines,
and they don't have time.

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:05.170
But why not read it
after you see the movie.

00:44:05.170 --> 00:44:09.310
Let the movie that you're
reviewing be the prime thing,

00:44:09.310 --> 00:44:12.380
and then read the book after it.

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:15.100
So, I think with
Jane Austen, it's

00:44:15.100 --> 00:44:18.610
dangerous changing too much.

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:20.410
But it's what people say.

00:44:20.410 --> 00:44:22.120
I mean, you can be
faithful to the spirit

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:23.920
totally in [INAUDIBLE].

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:28.240
One of the really outrageous
adaptations I've seen

00:44:28.240 --> 00:44:40.230
is the film version of the
Evelyn Waugh, the sentimental,

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:43.730
Evelyn Waugh, Brideshead, yeah.

00:44:43.730 --> 00:44:47.870
So, it was a beautiful,
absolutely beautiful mini

00:44:47.870 --> 00:44:51.830
series with Jeremy Irons.

00:44:51.830 --> 00:44:54.110
And they redid it as a film.

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:55.160
I don't know why.

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:58.080
And they completely reversed
everything about it.

00:44:58.080 --> 00:44:59.180
And why do that?

00:44:59.180 --> 00:45:06.440
I mean, it's imposing
whatever your own little angle

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:07.640
is on to material.

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:09.200
Do different material.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:11.537
Don't do something that
you're going to traduce.

00:45:11.537 --> 00:45:12.870
So, thank you for that question.

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:14.110
It's great.

00:45:14.110 --> 00:45:17.200
MODERATOR: Question
to speaker's left.

00:45:17.200 --> 00:45:19.390
AUDIENCE: You, sir,
so much for your talk.

00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:22.420
My question is, you talked
a lot about how we have so

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:24.160
much material with Jane Austen.

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:25.960
And we have so many
adaptations of it,

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:28.150
and we might grow tired of it.

00:45:28.150 --> 00:45:30.720
And then a few years ago
there was an adaptation

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:32.470
of Pride and Prejudice
that took liberties

00:45:32.470 --> 00:45:34.810
by adding zombies into the mix.

00:45:34.810 --> 00:45:37.090
And so, my question
is do you think

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:39.250
that is an indication
of the fact

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:42.722
that we're tired of Jane
Austen and we need things

00:45:42.722 --> 00:45:43.930
to make it more entertaining?

00:45:43.930 --> 00:45:46.690
Or is it an example of
something that people

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:49.255
are being super creative
with Jane Austen's material?

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:54.830
WHIT STILLMAN: I know the
director of that film.

00:45:54.830 --> 00:45:59.720
In fact, he was an
actor in the third film

00:45:59.720 --> 00:46:01.820
we did, The Last Days of Disco.

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:05.450
He played the door Nazi, not
letting people in the disco.

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:06.417
It's a great guy.

00:46:06.417 --> 00:46:08.000
And he said he wanted
to take the part

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:09.208
so he could study indie film.

00:46:09.208 --> 00:46:11.570
And he has done
really, really well.

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:15.140
Maybe that isn't
the best one of--

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:16.981
and he cast three of
our actresses in it.

00:46:16.981 --> 00:46:19.273
It was a little frustrating
because, Morfydd Clark, who

00:46:19.273 --> 00:46:22.040
I think is a brilliant actress,
who was the first person

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:24.350
cast in the film, who
plays Frederica-- and is

00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:25.490
going on to big things.

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:28.740
She's going on to the big
Tolkien TV series and things.

00:46:28.740 --> 00:46:31.590
And she's already
done tremendous work.

00:46:31.590 --> 00:46:36.022
And so we cast her for
her first film offer.

00:46:36.022 --> 00:46:38.480
But we weren't ready to film
because we were still looking.

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:40.147
We hadn't found the
Hillsdale investors.

00:46:40.147 --> 00:46:42.738
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:42.738 --> 00:46:44.530
Everyone made tons of
money, I have to say.

00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:45.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:46.480
And, sorry.

00:46:46.480 --> 00:46:49.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:53.470
And so, she was in that
film before our film,

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:55.150
and she's just
doing tons of stuff.

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:02.494
Was that the question?

00:47:02.494 --> 00:47:02.994
I mean--

00:47:02.994 --> 00:47:03.907
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:03.907 --> 00:47:05.240
I've got a lot more I could say.

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:10.830
AUDIENCE: So you
mentioned film adap--

00:47:10.830 --> 00:47:12.480
I mean obviously,
film adaptations,

00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:13.522
what this talk was about.

00:47:13.522 --> 00:47:16.890
But Sense and Sensibility
specifically, I believe,

00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:19.530
was what you said
was your favorite

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:21.360
or the one you
respected the most.

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:24.330
And I was just curious as
to why you consider that

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:27.380
the best Austen Adaptation?

00:47:27.380 --> 00:47:30.110
WHIT STILLMAN: I mean I
haven't seen every Austen

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:33.298
adaptation because some I
just can't bear to watch them.

00:47:33.298 --> 00:47:35.840
But maybe they get really good
after the first three minutes.

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:38.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:46.470
But I think that they're
aided there by the fact

00:47:46.470 --> 00:47:50.620
that it's the lesser of
the five great novels.

00:47:50.620 --> 00:47:54.120
And that it was a
great novel for me

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:56.120
to read first because
I was sort of coming

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:58.620
from the world of nonfiction,
of reading literary criticism,

00:47:58.620 --> 00:47:59.940
and not the novels.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:04.240
And it's the most essay-ish,
most like an essay.

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:09.850
And I think that allowed them
to sort of take that and--

00:48:09.850 --> 00:48:11.850
I mean there are just so
many beautiful elements

00:48:11.850 --> 00:48:12.690
in that film.

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:15.810
I'm not really a critic,
so I can't really say.

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:16.930
But, I don't know.

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:19.407
I think it's generally--

00:48:19.407 --> 00:48:20.740
I think that's the general view.

00:48:20.740 --> 00:48:24.790
I think it's generally the
best regarded adaptation.

00:48:24.790 --> 00:48:27.100
And to get back to
Pride and Prejudice

00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:30.520
and Zombies, if I didn't
finish that, I think

00:48:30.520 --> 00:48:33.190
it'd be great if there was
just sort of a fallow period

00:48:33.190 --> 00:48:35.860
and like not so
much of Jane Austen,

00:48:35.860 --> 00:48:37.930
or Brigerton's,
or whatever it is,

00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:42.280
and that we just allowed to come
back in a decade or five years

00:48:42.280 --> 00:48:44.650
and try to do really great
versions of all the others

00:48:44.650 --> 00:48:46.430
that haven't been done,
as great versions.

00:48:46.430 --> 00:48:49.420
And I think it really would be
great to think about someone

00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:52.360
like Trollope and how
a feature film could

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:54.460
be made of Trollope novels.

00:48:54.460 --> 00:48:56.025
Because a lot of
the same dynamic

00:48:56.025 --> 00:48:57.400
that people
appreciated-- there's

00:48:57.400 --> 00:49:00.970
a lot of morality, really the
kind of exquisite morality

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:03.130
and humor in Trollope.

00:49:03.130 --> 00:49:08.207
It's not works of art at the
same level of Jane Austen.

00:49:08.207 --> 00:49:09.790
But there's no need
to compare and say

00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:11.415
one's better than
another and all that.

00:49:11.415 --> 00:49:15.100
It's just I'm just so excited
to find all these great Trollope

00:49:15.100 --> 00:49:16.120
novels that I'm reading.

00:49:18.547 --> 00:49:20.630
AUDIENCE: You briefly
mentioned The Cosmopolitans.

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:22.860
So is there an update on that?

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:24.830
WHIT STILLMAN: Well, I
think it might be going

00:49:24.830 --> 00:49:28.130
in sort of two directions.

00:49:28.130 --> 00:49:29.990
I hope to take
some elements of it

00:49:29.990 --> 00:49:31.860
and put it into a
different kind of series.

00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:36.140
So, I think things will
come out of Cosmopolitans.

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:39.350
But it probably won't
be directly continuing

00:49:39.350 --> 00:49:41.850
exactly that.

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:44.720
And if anyone needs a link,
I can get links to people.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:47.528
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:47.528 --> 00:49:49.820
AUDIENCE: So, thank you so
much for your excellent film

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:52.020
and your explanation
of it to us tonight.

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:55.190
I'm curious, especially
after her poignant portrayal

00:49:55.190 --> 00:49:57.350
in your film, could
you perhaps offer

00:49:57.350 --> 00:50:02.130
a summary of your
vindication for Lady Susan?

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:05.280
WHIT STILLMAN: Well,
[CLEARS THROAT] funny you ask.

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:09.200
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:11.980
How many minutes do I have?

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:13.480
OK, I'll quickly read this.

00:50:13.480 --> 00:50:14.810
OK, this is the opening.

00:50:14.810 --> 00:50:17.470
So, this novel--

00:50:17.470 --> 00:50:22.570
So, Sir James Martin, Tom
Bennett, that comic performance

00:50:22.570 --> 00:50:28.780
really inspired me and also
a very kind of comical memoir

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:31.870
of Jane Austen by her nephew.

00:50:31.870 --> 00:50:35.410
So I thought, what if Lady
Susan and Sir James Martin

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:40.870
had a nephew who wanted to do
a defense of his aunt the way

00:50:40.870 --> 00:50:43.870
her nephew did a
defense of his aunt?

00:50:43.870 --> 00:50:47.590
So, anyway, this fellow
turns everything around.

00:50:47.590 --> 00:50:49.480
Let me just read as
much of this as I can.

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:56.120
It's the opening of Love and
Friendship, Jane Austen's Lady

00:50:56.120 --> 00:50:58.460
Susan Vernon
Entirely Vindicated.

00:50:58.460 --> 00:50:59.540
That's the novel.

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:01.737
Because by the time
I've done a film,

00:51:01.737 --> 00:51:03.320
I don't want the
novel to be the film.

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:05.160
I want the novel to be
something else again.

00:51:05.160 --> 00:51:06.830
And so, this is sort of--

00:51:06.830 --> 00:51:10.550
anyway, a true narrative
of false witness.

00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:12.290
They who bear false
witness against

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:15.170
the innocent and blameless
are rightly condemned.

00:51:15.170 --> 00:51:17.960
What though of they who bear
false witness against those

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:20.240
whose histories
are not spotless?

00:51:20.240 --> 00:51:22.100
To commit one sin
or indiscretion

00:51:22.100 --> 00:51:24.500
is not to commit every
sin or indiscretion.

00:51:24.500 --> 00:51:26.420
Yet many speak as if it were.

00:51:26.420 --> 00:51:28.430
Such was the case of
the DeCourcy family

00:51:28.430 --> 00:51:30.290
of Parkland's
Kent, who disguised

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:33.500
their prideful arrogance,
indefensible in our faith

00:51:33.500 --> 00:51:36.140
under the cloak of moral
nicety and correction.

00:51:36.140 --> 00:51:38.060
As so often with
our aristocracy,

00:51:38.060 --> 00:51:40.700
the DeCourcy's these did not
conduct their soiling vendettas

00:51:40.700 --> 00:51:41.690
themselves.

00:51:41.690 --> 00:51:44.310
But the truth is sycophants and
hangers-on on in their circle,

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:46.550
in this case, the
spinster authoress,

00:51:46.550 --> 00:51:48.307
notorious for her
poisen-pen fictions

00:51:48.307 --> 00:51:50.015
hidden under their
lambskin of anonymity.

00:51:50.015 --> 00:51:51.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:53.930
That other less prominent
character assassinations

00:51:53.930 --> 00:51:57.110
later joined the cruel fray does
not lessen that lady's singular

00:51:57.110 --> 00:51:58.160
culpability.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:00.800
Having myself been the
target of such slanders,

00:52:00.800 --> 00:52:02.600
I well know the
near impossibility

00:52:02.600 --> 00:52:05.480
of cleansing one's good
name from their aspersions.

00:52:05.480 --> 00:52:07.490
And if one's name
is not so good,

00:52:07.490 --> 00:52:10.190
that near impossibility
becomes even nearer.

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:12.110
Whatever one answers,
no matter how true

00:52:12.110 --> 00:52:14.330
and well evidence the
defense or explanation,

00:52:14.330 --> 00:52:18.230
one is forever besmirched,
even irrefutable denials.

00:52:18.230 --> 00:52:19.940
This applies to a
lot of my friends,

00:52:19.940 --> 00:52:22.940
who've been in the film
business and got called out

00:52:22.940 --> 00:52:23.860
for various things.

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:24.080
[LAUGHS]

00:52:24.080 --> 00:52:24.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:24.580 --> 00:52:27.680
Anyway, serve only
to further circulate

00:52:27.680 --> 00:52:29.210
the original slanders.

00:52:29.210 --> 00:52:31.550
If defending one's
reputation is difficult

00:52:31.550 --> 00:52:34.670
when one is still of this world
surrounded by one's papers,

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:37.100
correspondence, calendars,
diaries and overdicture

00:52:37.100 --> 00:52:39.260
of every kind,
with memories still

00:52:39.260 --> 00:52:42.440
lively to marshal in one's
own defense, how much harder

00:52:42.440 --> 00:52:44.810
after one has departed it?

00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:46.880
Lady Susan Grey
Vernon was my aunt,

00:52:46.880 --> 00:52:49.340
and the kindest, most
delightful woman anyone

00:52:49.340 --> 00:52:53.300
could know, a shining ornament
to our society and nation.

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.240
I am convinced that the
insinuations and accusations

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:58.790
made against her are
nearly, entirely false.

00:52:58.790 --> 00:52:59.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:59.780 --> 00:53:02.210
I have taken as my
sacred obligation

00:53:02.210 --> 00:53:05.390
the task of convincing
the world of this also.

00:53:05.390 --> 00:53:07.160
Sir Arthur Helps,
the great biographer

00:53:07.160 --> 00:53:09.710
of Prince Henry, the
Navigator, heroic initiator

00:53:09.710 --> 00:53:12.740
of the Age of Discovery,
has described that prince's

00:53:12.740 --> 00:53:14.450
not-uncommon motive.

00:53:14.450 --> 00:53:17.030
A man sees something
that ought to be done,

00:53:17.030 --> 00:53:19.460
knows of no one that
will do it but himself,

00:53:19.460 --> 00:53:22.100
and so is driven
to the enterprise.

00:53:22.100 --> 00:53:25.010
Anyway, I think that's
done with all our time.

00:53:25.010 --> 00:53:25.510
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:25.510 --> 00:53:26.570
Thank you.

00:53:26.570 --> 00:53:27.230
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:27.230 --> 00:53:27.830
Thank you.

00:53:27.830 --> 00:53:30.722
May you all be driven to the
right enterprises and not

00:53:30.722 --> 00:53:31.550
the wrong ones.

00:53:31.550 --> 00:53:33.020
MODERATOR: Thank
you, Mr. Stillman.

00:53:33.020 --> 00:53:33.590
Thank you.

00:53:33.590 --> 00:53:37.240
[APPLAUSE]

